    phase (5e):`). A dispatch that halted, timed out or was rejected authored nothing. The agent of\n    a dispatch is the value of the `TDD-IMPLEMENTER:` token the orchestrator resolved just before\n    staging it, which is the `--agent` it passed to `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` (`--phase green` for\n    the 5e GREEN dispatch; `--phase` takes `red` or `green`); for the Claude fallback, where no\n    dispatch leaves the session, it is the same token's `claude` value.\n  - **The durable author record (v1.3, OD-E2 option (a), spec FR-1 D-15).** Each accepted\n    implementation dispatch is recorded as one `implementation_authors` entry, through the same\n    `--append` operation as above:\n    `--append implementation_authors='{\"module\":\"<module>\",\"agent\":\"<agent>\"}'`. The writer gains\n    no code for it beyond FR-1's schema entry and the `APPEND_ONLY` refusal: `--append` is\n    key-agnostic, and `classify` applies the item schema on both validator paths.\n  - **Where in the procedure the append happens.** The rule over the axis is an ordering:\n    **every accepted implementation dispatch for a module is appended before any review leg of\n    its code runs** (spec FR-1 D-15), and so before that leg's `review_independence` entry. Its\n    members in the procedure:\n    - **5e GREEN.** In SKILL.md's 5e bullet, when the orchestrator accepts a module's GREEN\n      dispatch, that is when it reads the `STATUS:` token `DONE` or `DONE_WITH_CONCERNS` and stops\n      re-dispatching GREEN for that module. It appends there, before the module's 5e spec-review\n      leg runs. A GREEN that is\n      re-dispatched (the path toward `step5e:green_unreachable:<module>`) is not accepted and is\n      not appended.\n    - **Every accepted fix dispatch** for the module, wherever it is dispatched (a 5e fix after a\n      review or verification finding, or a fix after a 6a-prime `WITH_FIXES`): appended once it\n      is accepted, before the next leg that reviews the fixed code.\n    - **Never 5d RED.** The 5d bullet gains no append.\n\n    An accepted GREEN that a later step halts on (`step5e:verify_failed:<module>`, a failed revert\n    test) keeps its entry, because entries are append-only and its code is still in the tree. That\n    direction is the safe one: an extra author can only turn an entry `independent=false`, never\n    `true`. Residual, exactly: the order is orchestrator prose (SKILL.md, FR-10), and no code\n    refuses a `review_independence` append made before the author append it should follow. A\n    refused author append (an invalid entry) prints the writer's `ERROR:` refusal with the store\n    unchanged; the orchestrator corrects the entry and appends again before the review leg runs\n    (spec FR-1).\n  - **Reading the complete author set.** For module `M`, the set is the `agent` values of the\n    well-formed entries of the record's `implementation_authors` whose `module == M`. Well-formed\n    is `property_valid(\"implementation_authors\", [entry])` (§\"The warnings\" below), the definition\n    the orchestrator applies, since no script reads the field (spec FR-1). The latest\n    accepted implementation dispatch is the last such entry in array order, since `--append` adds\n    at the end and the orchestrator appends at acceptance. The set is read from this field and\n    from nothing else: never from earlier `review_independence` entries, which are a projection of\n    it with one `implementer` per entry. Counter-example: with authors `[agy, codex]`, a grok\n    5e-review stores `implementer=codex` and a Codex 5e-verify stores `implementer=codex`, so a\n    set rebuilt from those entries is `{codex}` and an agy 6a-prime would read as independent\n    (design audit cycle 2, codex). The orchestrator's in-context list of dispatches is not read\n    either: the record is the only source, so a resumed orchestrator reads exactly what an\n    uninterrupted one reads. v1.2 kept the set \"in the procedure\" and is replaced by this.\n  - **A 5e leg** reviews one module. If the reviewer is in that module's author set,\n    `implementer` is the reviewer and `independent` is `false`. Otherwise `implementer` is the\n    agent of the module's latest entry, and `independent` is `true` (spec v1.8 FR-7, S5).\n  - **6a-prime** reviews the whole diff. The orchestrator writes one 6a-prime entry per module,\n    with `module` set, applying the same rule to that module's complete author set.\n  - **When no author can be read, no entry is written.** The leg writes no `review_independence`\n    entry for module `M`, rather than one whose `independent` it cannot establish, in exactly\n    these cases:\n    - the record has no `implementation_authors` field: a record whose implementation dispatches\n      were accepted before this field existed (spec v1.8 FR-7's \"no entry\" rule, kept for those\n      records only). An absent field is never read as an empty author set, which would make\n      every reviewer independent; AC-7.3's pre-field fixture pins this (spec v1.9);\n    - the field is present and holds no well-formed entry for `M`: an author append that was\n      skipped, the residual of the ordering rule above;\n    - the field is present and is not a list, or holds an entry that is not well-formed: a hand\n      edit, since the writer refuses both. One malformed entry may be the lost record of `M`'s\n      author, so the set is not established for any module of that record.\n\n    A resumed orchestrator whose dispatch context is gone is **not** a member: it reads the field\n    like any other. Residual, as the spec states: `review_independence_unrecorded` fires only when\n    no `leg=6a-prime` entry exists at all, so one module missing among several recorded ones has\n    no warning of its own.\n  - **AC-7.3's multi-author recovered fixture, from durable state.** The record holds\n    `implementation_authors` `[{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"agy\"},{\"module\":\"M\",\"agent\":\"codex\"}]`. A\n","total_lines":1489}},"locations":[]}
{"type":"tool_call_update","toolCallId":"call-07b964bb-3010-413c-99c8-05ac9938675c-16","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"2639→- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n2640→  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n2650→  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n2660→  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n2670→    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n2680→  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n2690→    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n2700→## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n2710→\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n2720→  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n2730→  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n2740→    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n2750→    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n2760→  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n2770→  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n2780→  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n2790→\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n2800→\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n2810→  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n2820→  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n2830→  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n2840→- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\nPaired audited plan: # Plan: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\n2850→Source: `docs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` v1.4, read at commit `e6610381` (v1.2 of\nthis plan; v1.1 read spec v1.3 at `e7730f78`).\nEvery \"currently\" claim below was read from the tree at `17d73037`, and the command that read it is\nwritten beside it. Those readings still hold at `e7730f78` for every `h-mad/` file:\n`git diff --stat 17d73037 e7730f78 -- h-mad` prints nothing. Claims first added in v1.1 are stamped\n`e7730f78`. Claims first added in v1.2 are stamped `d90decc6`, and the older readings still hold\nthere for every `h-mad/` file: `git diff --stat 17d73037 d90decc6 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\nv1.5 read spec v1.6 at `c853b207` and design v1.0 at `df46f72a`; its new readings are stamped\n`200ffd52`, and `git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad` prints nothing. v1.6 mirrors spec\nv1.7 at `c117a695` (D-12, D-13, D-14); `git diff --stat 200ffd52 c117a695 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\n2860→Commands run from the repository root unless they start with `cd`.\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nMake the Phase-5 implementer a per-feature choice between two peers, `codex` and `agy`. One\nresolution function in the TDD judge decides who authors. Claude's gate refuses for whichever peer\nis resolved. A new agy write gate enforces test-first on agy's own tools. Every code-review leg\nrecords whether its reviewer was also the implementer, and Phase 7 surfaces that for every\nimplementer, Codex included.\n\n2870→## Overview\n\nToday Codex is the only Phase-5 implementer h-mad accepts. The state schema, Claude's\nauthorship gate, the dispatch assembler and the implementer prompt all hard-code it, and agy has no\nwrite gate at all (spec §\"Problem\"). The operator wants agy usable whenever Codex is unavailable,\nand selectable per feature, with no loss of the test-first guarantee and with any automatic switch\nmade visible. agy is already installed and already dispatched headless by\n`hmad-dispatch exec agy` for review legs. `agy --version` → `1.4.0` at `200ffd52` (it read\n`1.3.6` at `17d73037`). This reading moves with every agy upgrade, so FR-8 and the `SessionStart`\nre-check stamp the version they ran against rather than citing this one.\n2880→\n## Scope\n\nIn scope, all under `h-mad/` unless noted:\n\n- **State**: four optional fields in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` and their rows in\n  `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-1).\n- **Resolution**: one function and one CLI verb, `implementer`, in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`\n  (FR-2).\n- **Claude-side gate**: `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` reads the resolution from the judge and refuses\n2890→  per peer (FR-3).\n- **agy write gate**: a new `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`, with its wiring check (FR-4, FR-5).\n- **Shared shell policy**: one module imported by both the Codex gate and the agy gate (FR-6).\n- **Review independence**: the state record and the Phase-7 warnings (FR-7).\n- **Live tracer**: a real agy RED/GREEN under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, which\n  also sets the `--agent agy` default timeout (FR-8).\n- **Prompt**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` becomes implementer-neutral and gains the\n  WIRE-PIN new-caller rule (FR-9).\n- **Docs**: SKILL.md, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` and `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-10).\n- **Per-project wiring**: a tracked `.agents/hooks.json` in each project that adopts the agy gate\n2900→  (D-2, D-10).\n\nUser-visible behaviour: the operator sets `implementer=codex|agy` on a feature record. The\norchestrator stages whichever peer resolves and prints any switch on the dispatch. A Phase-7 report\nmay now carry `review_non_independent`, `review_independence_unrecorded` or\n`review_independence_malformed` warnings.\n\n## Goals\n\n- **G1 (FR-1).** The schema declares `implementer`, `agy_status`, `implementer_switch` and\n2910→  `review_independence` as optional. Records written before this feature validate unchanged.\n- **G2 (FR-2).** Exactly one function resolves who authors a record. Both the gate and the\n  `implementer` verb read it, and neither re-derives any part of it.\n- **G3 (FR-3).** Claude's gate refuses for the resolved peer (`codex-authorship` /\n  `agy-authorship`). It refuses `implementer-invalid` first. The `fallback_agent` fold fires only\n  when both peers are out. The three existing message texts stay byte-identical.\n- **G4 (FR-4, FR-6).** agy cannot write a production `.py` without a failing test during active\n  step5, through any tool. Tools the gate does not know are denied, and the shell gets Codex's\n  command-shape rule from the same implementation.\n- **G5 (FR-5).** A project that lacks the wiring halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` before any\n2920→  agy prompt is written. It never passes silently.\n- **G6 (FR-7; D-9, D-11).** Every code-review leg records whether it was independent, for **every**\n  implementer (`codex|agy|grok|claude`). Phase 7 warns on a non-independent leg, on a malformed\n  entry, and — whoever implemented — on a 6a-prime leg that ran with no `leg=6a-prime` entry. These\n  are warnings only, never blockers.\n- **G7 (FR-8).** A real refusal by agy is observed, a real agy RED/GREEN passes the same evidence\n  bar as Codex, and the default timeout is derived from measured turns.\n- **G8 (FR-9, FR-10).** The prompt and the docs stop telling a non-Codex implementer it is Codex,\n  and a valid new-caller RED is no longer halted as `red_wrong_reason`.\n\n2930→## Requirements\n\nThis plan satisfies FR-1 through FR-10 of the spec, with the 19 acceptance criteria AC-1.1, AC-1.2,\nAC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-6.1, AC-7.1, AC-7.2, AC-8.1,\nAC-8.2, AC-9.1, AC-9.2, AC-9.3 and AC-9.4, plus AC-X (`python3 h-mad/scripts/h_mad_ac_census.py\ndocs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` → `ACS: OK count=19 frs=9` at `e8def13f`, spec v1.5, and the same at `c117a695`, spec v1.7). The\ndecisions D-1..D-14 are inputs, not open questions. Spec v1.7 (`c117a695`) adds three, each\nmirrored where it binds:\n\n- **D-12.** The FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not\n2940→  a new module (was the design's OQ-A). Stage 4, DO-5.\n- **D-13.** FR-8 is a post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded\n  COMPLETE only after it passes, with AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 (was the design's OQ-B). Stage 8, DO-6, R-2.\n- **D-14.** `implementer_switch.ts` is narrowed to seconds `00`–`59`; a leap second is refused on\n  both validator paths, because h-mad's writer produces `.ts` through Python `datetime`, which\n  cannot hold second 60. Stage 1.\n\nTwo of the earlier decisions must be carried exactly as the spec states them, because the\nbrainstorm's framing was narrower:\n\n2950→- **D-9.** The non-independent flag fires whenever `reviewer == implementer`, Codex reviewing Codex\n  included (for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex on a Codex feature).\n- **D-11.** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires for **every** implementer. Its trigger is that\n  a 6a-prime leg ran (`archreview` is `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` or `NO`) and there is no\n  `leg=6a-prime` entry. No grandfather rule applies. No surface this feature writes may restate\n  that trigger as agy-only.\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n**Order of work.** Each stage depends only on the stages before it. Each stage is one or more\n2960→impl-plan Tasks, and the full h-mad suite runs per Task (see §\"Success Criteria\").\n\n1. **Schema and writer (FR-1).** Declare the four fields. Also rewrite the `fallback_agent`\n   `description` in `h_mad_state_schema.json` and its row in `state-schema.md`. Both currently tie\n   the field to Codex alone: the schema says it \"is read only when codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n 'read only when codex is out' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`), and the\n   reference row says \"Optional Phase-5 author when Codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n '^| .fallback_agent' h-mad/references/state-schema.md`). After FR-3 both are false:\n   the field is read only when **both** peers are out. Spec v1.3 FR-1 now lists both surfaces.\n   The schema description is pinned:\n2970→   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\n   asserts four substrings, `read only when codex is out`, `are equivalent`,\n   `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE does not override` and `prose-enforced`\n   (`grep -n -A 14 'def test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py`\n   at `e7730f78`). The rewrite keeps all four, or changes that test to the new fact in the same\n   commit. The test is never deleted. The Task's RED names which of the two it does.\n   - **The `codex_status` description and row are rewritten too (spec v1.7 FR-1, AC-1.1), on\n     both surfaces.** The schema `description` says `exhausted`/`unavailable` records that Codex\n     cannot author, \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\", and the `state-schema.md` row says\n     \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\" (at `c117a695`,\n2980→     `grep -c 'permitting Claude to write the fallback' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` → 1\n     line and `grep -c 'permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback' h-mad/references/state-schema.md` → 1\n     line). Both are false once agy is available. The rewrite states authorship as FR-2's\n     resolution, Claude only when `resolved` = `claude`, and leaves the audit-routing half\n     unchanged. The Task's RED pins one sentence on each surface. No test pins the old text today:\n     `git ls-files -z h-mad/tests | xargs -0 grep -l 'permitting Claude to write the fallback\\|permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\\|One switch, both effects\\|blocked when Codex is available' | wc -l`\n     → 0 files at `c117a695`. That zero is load-bearing only for this rewrite, so the Task re-runs\n     the grep. `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` pins property ORDER\n     (`fallback_agent` directly after `codex_status`), so the FR-1 fields are never inserted\n     between the two.\n2990→   - **AC-1.2's leap-second fixture (D-14).** `implementer_switch.ts` = `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` is\n     its own fixture, refused on the library path and, under the existing seam that forces the\n     bundled validator, on the bundled path, one run per path. The writer's form\n     `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both. The narrowing refuses nothing the writer can\n     produce: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -I -c 'from datetime import datetime; datetime.fromisoformat(\"2016-12-31T23:59:60+00:00\")'`\n     raises `ValueError: second must be in 0..59` at `c117a695`.\n2. **Resolution function and the `implementer` verb (FR-2).** The env-var and `PATH` evaluation\n   moves out of the hook into the judge. The `--record` write goes through\n   `h_mad_state_write.py`'s write path.\n3. **Claude-side gate on the new state line (FR-3).** This rewrites the hook's state parse and\n3000→   fold, which is where the existing mutation anchors sit (see §\"Risks\"). The same Task applies\n   the AC-3.2 exception (DO-1a): it re-shapes the stub literals in the two named gate test files,\n   updates `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE`, and adds the fixture showing that a\n   today-shape line refuses `judge-error`. The new line must also satisfy the placement\n   invariants P-1..P-5 in DO-1a. Those are assertions on the judge's **real** output, not on stubs,\n   so re-shaping a stub cannot satisfy them. The Task's RED names each one it expects to hold.\n4. **Shared shell-policy module (FR-6).** This is a pure move out of\n   `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`. The destination is `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`,\n   not a new module: D-12, the operator decision on the design's OQ-A (design v1.0 §\"FR-6\"), because a new module the gate\n   imports breaks a hand-built test tree that FR-6 says passes unmodified. Codex-gate behaviour does not change. Every moved name\n3010→   that an existing test reaches stays bound on the Codex gate module, by import.\n   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command` at 2 sites\n   (`grep -n '_safe_shell_command' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` → 2 matching\n   lines at `e7730f78`). The mutation rows in the moved code (M-4) are re-pointed to the new\n   module in the same Task. Then each spec that holds one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n5. **agy gate (FR-4), then its wiring check and assembler enforcement (FR-5).** The gate imports\n   the module from stage 4 and the judge from stage 2. **Exit codes follow the audit-gate signal\n   discipline** (`h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Audit-gate signal discipline\": a normal verdict,\n   PASS or FAIL, prints its token and exits 0; nonzero only for an operational error). Spec v1.4\n   FR-5's exit-code table is the contract, and this plan mirrors it without restating the table:\n3020→   - **Verdicts exit 0.** `AGY-GATE: WIRED` and every `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED reason=…` (all seven\n     reasons, `no-hooks-file` and `self-check-failed` included) exit 0 from the check. The\n     assembler's `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` on `UNWIRED` writes no prompt and\n     exits 0.\n   - **Operational errors exit 2.** `AGY-GATE: UNREADABLE` exits 2 from the check, and the\n     assembler HALT exits 2. A check that prints no `AGY-GATE:` token gives\n     `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token` with exit 2.\n   - The orchestrator halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` in every non-`WIRED` row. Callers\n     read the token, never `$?`.\n   - **Precedent and divergence.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` returns 0 on `WIRING: FAIL` and 2 on\n3030→     `WIRING: UNKNOWN reason=no_settings`\n     (`grep -n 'return [02]' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_hook_wiring.py` at `d90decc6`). The agy check\n     treats a missing `hooks.json` as a verdict instead, deliberately, per spec v1.4 FR-5.\n   - **The assembler then carries two conventions.** Its module docstring says\n     `exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT`\n     (`grep -n '2 on any HALT' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` at `d90decc6`), and its\n     existing HALTs keep exit 2 (spec v1.4 FR-5 residual). The FR-5 Task rewrites that docstring\n     in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, in the same commit, to say which HALT exits which way. A\n     grep-able sentence pins the rewrite, so a stale \"2 on any HALT\" fails a test."}}],"rawOutput":{"type":"ReadFile","FileContent":{"content":"2639→- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n2640→  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n2650→  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n2660→  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n2670→    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n2680→  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n2690→    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n2700→## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n2710→\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n2720→  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n2730→  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n2740→    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n2750→    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n2760→  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n2770→  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n2780→  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n2790→\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n2800→\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n2810→  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n2820→  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n2830→  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n2840→- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\nPaired audited plan: # Plan: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\n2850→Source: `docs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` v1.4, read at commit `e6610381` (v1.2 of\nthis plan; v1.1 read spec v1.3 at `e7730f78`).\nEvery \"currently\" claim below was read from the tree at `17d73037`, and the command that read it is\nwritten beside it. Those readings still hold at `e7730f78` for every `h-mad/` file:\n`git diff --stat 17d73037 e7730f78 -- h-mad` prints nothing. Claims first added in v1.1 are stamped\n`e7730f78`. Claims first added in v1.2 are stamped `d90decc6`, and the older readings still hold\nthere for every `h-mad/` file: `git diff --stat 17d73037 d90decc6 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\nv1.5 read spec v1.6 at `c853b207` and design v1.0 at `df46f72a`; its new readings are stamped\n`200ffd52`, and `git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad` prints nothing. v1.6 mirrors spec\nv1.7 at `c117a695` (D-12, D-13, D-14); `git diff --stat 200ffd52 c117a695 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\n2860→Commands run from the repository root unless they start with `cd`.\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nMake the Phase-5 implementer a per-feature choice between two peers, `codex` and `agy`. One\nresolution function in the TDD judge decides who authors. Claude's gate refuses for whichever peer\nis resolved. A new agy write gate enforces test-first on agy's own tools. Every code-review leg\nrecords whether its reviewer was also the implementer, and Phase 7 surfaces that for every\nimplementer, Codex included.\n\n2870→## Overview\n\nToday Codex is the only Phase-5 implementer h-mad accepts. The state schema, Claude's\nauthorship gate, the dispatch assembler and the implementer prompt all hard-code it, and agy has no\nwrite gate at all (spec §\"Problem\"). The operator wants agy usable whenever Codex is unavailable,\nand selectable per feature, with no loss of the test-first guarantee and with any automatic switch\nmade visible. agy is already installed and already dispatched headless by\n`hmad-dispatch exec agy` for review legs. `agy --version` → `1.4.0` at `200ffd52` (it read\n`1.3.6` at `17d73037`). This reading moves with every agy upgrade, so FR-8 and the `SessionStart`\nre-check stamp the version they ran against rather than citing this one.\n2880→\n## Scope\n\nIn scope, all under `h-mad/` unless noted:\n\n- **State**: four optional fields in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` and their rows in\n  `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-1).\n- **Resolution**: one function and one CLI verb, `implementer`, in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`\n  (FR-2).\n- **Claude-side gate**: `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` reads the resolution from the judge and refuses\n2890→  per peer (FR-3).\n- **agy write gate**: a new `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`, with its wiring check (FR-4, FR-5).\n- **Shared shell policy**: one module imported by both the Codex gate and the agy gate (FR-6).\n- **Review independence**: the state record and the Phase-7 warnings (FR-7).\n- **Live tracer**: a real agy RED/GREEN under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, which\n  also sets the `--agent agy` default timeout (FR-8).\n- **Prompt**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` becomes implementer-neutral and gains the\n  WIRE-PIN new-caller rule (FR-9).\n- **Docs**: SKILL.md, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` and `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-10).\n- **Per-project wiring**: a tracked `.agents/hooks.json` in each project that adopts the agy gate\n2900→  (D-2, D-10).\n\nUser-visible behaviour: the operator sets `implementer=codex|agy` on a feature record. The\norchestrator stages whichever peer resolves and prints any switch on the dispatch. A Phase-7 report\nmay now carry `review_non_independent`, `review_independence_unrecorded` or\n`review_independence_malformed` warnings.\n\n## Goals\n\n- **G1 (FR-1).** The schema declares `implementer`, `agy_status`, `implementer_switch` and\n2910→  `review_independence` as optional. Records written before this feature validate unchanged.\n- **G2 (FR-2).** Exactly one function resolves who authors a record. Both the gate and the\n  `implementer` verb read it, and neither re-derives any part of it.\n- **G3 (FR-3).** Claude's gate refuses for the resolved peer (`codex-authorship` /\n  `agy-authorship`). It refuses `implementer-invalid` first. The `fallback_agent` fold fires only\n  when both peers are out. The three existing message texts stay byte-identical.\n- **G4 (FR-4, FR-6).** agy cannot write a production `.py` without a failing test during active\n  step5, through any tool. Tools the gate does not know are denied, and the shell gets Codex's\n  command-shape rule from the same implementation.\n- **G5 (FR-5).** A project that lacks the wiring halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` before any\n2920→  agy prompt is written. It never passes silently.\n- **G6 (FR-7; D-9, D-11).** Every code-review leg records whether it was independent, for **every**\n  implementer (`codex|agy|grok|claude`). Phase 7 warns on a non-independent leg, on a malformed\n  entry, and — whoever implemented — on a 6a-prime leg that ran with no `leg=6a-prime` entry. These\n  are warnings only, never blockers.\n- **G7 (FR-8).** A real refusal by agy is observed, a real agy RED/GREEN passes the same evidence\n  bar as Codex, and the default timeout is derived from measured turns.\n- **G8 (FR-9, FR-10).** The prompt and the docs stop telling a non-Codex implementer it is Codex,\n  and a valid new-caller RED is no longer halted as `red_wrong_reason`.\n\n2930→## Requirements\n\nThis plan satisfies FR-1 through FR-10 of the spec, with the 19 acceptance criteria AC-1.1, AC-1.2,\nAC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-6.1, AC-7.1, AC-7.2, AC-8.1,\nAC-8.2, AC-9.1, AC-9.2, AC-9.3 and AC-9.4, plus AC-X (`python3 h-mad/scripts/h_mad_ac_census.py\ndocs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` → `ACS: OK count=19 frs=9` at `e8def13f`, spec v1.5, and the same at `c117a695`, spec v1.7). The\ndecisions D-1..D-14 are inputs, not open questions. Spec v1.7 (`c117a695`) adds three, each\nmirrored where it binds:\n\n- **D-12.** The FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not\n2940→  a new module (was the design's OQ-A). Stage 4, DO-5.\n- **D-13.** FR-8 is a post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded\n  COMPLETE only after it passes, with AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 (was the design's OQ-B). Stage 8, DO-6, R-2.\n- **D-14.** `implementer_switch.ts` is narrowed to seconds `00`–`59`; a leap second is refused on\n  both validator paths, because h-mad's writer produces `.ts` through Python `datetime`, which\n  cannot hold second 60. Stage 1.\n\nTwo of the earlier decisions must be carried exactly as the spec states them, because the\nbrainstorm's framing was narrower:\n\n2950→- **D-9.** The non-independent flag fires whenever `reviewer == implementer`, Codex reviewing Codex\n  included (for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex on a Codex feature).\n- **D-11.** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires for **every** implementer. Its trigger is that\n  a 6a-prime leg ran (`archreview` is `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` or `NO`) and there is no\n  `leg=6a-prime` entry. No grandfather rule applies. No surface this feature writes may restate\n  that trigger as agy-only.\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n**Order of work.** Each stage depends only on the stages before it. Each stage is one or more\n2960→impl-plan Tasks, and the full h-mad suite runs per Task (see §\"Success Criteria\").\n\n1. **Schema and writer (FR-1).** Declare the four fields. Also rewrite the `fallback_agent`\n   `description` in `h_mad_state_schema.json` and its row in `state-schema.md`. Both currently tie\n   the field to Codex alone: the schema says it \"is read only when codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n 'read only when codex is out' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`), and the\n   reference row says \"Optional Phase-5 author when Codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n '^| .fallback_agent' h-mad/references/state-schema.md`). After FR-3 both are false:\n   the field is read only when **both** peers are out. Spec v1.3 FR-1 now lists both surfaces.\n   The schema description is pinned:\n2970→   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\n   asserts four substrings, `read only when codex is out`, `are equivalent`,\n   `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE does not override` and `prose-enforced`\n   (`grep -n -A 14 'def test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py`\n   at `e7730f78`). The rewrite keeps all four, or changes that test to the new fact in the same\n   commit. The test is never deleted. The Task's RED names which of the two it does.\n   - **The `codex_status` description and row are rewritten too (spec v1.7 FR-1, AC-1.1), on\n     both surfaces.** The schema `description` says `exhausted`/`unavailable` records that Codex\n     cannot author, \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\", and the `state-schema.md` row says\n     \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\" (at `c117a695`,\n2980→     `grep -c 'permitting Claude to write the fallback' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` → 1\n     line and `grep -c 'permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback' h-mad/references/state-schema.md` → 1\n     line). Both are false once agy is available. The rewrite states authorship as FR-2's\n     resolution, Claude only when `resolved` = `claude`, and leaves the audit-routing half\n     unchanged. The Task's RED pins one sentence on each surface. No test pins the old text today:\n     `git ls-files -z h-mad/tests | xargs -0 grep -l 'permitting Claude to write the fallback\\|permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\\|One switch, both effects\\|blocked when Codex is available' | wc -l`\n     → 0 files at `c117a695`. That zero is load-bearing only for this rewrite, so the Task re-runs\n     the grep. `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` pins property ORDER\n     (`fallback_agent` directly after `codex_status`), so the FR-1 fields are never inserted\n     between the two.\n2990→   - **AC-1.2's leap-second fixture (D-14).** `implementer_switch.ts` = `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` is\n     its own fixture, refused on the library path and, under the existing seam that forces the\n     bundled validator, on the bundled path, one run per path. The writer's form\n     `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both. The narrowing refuses nothing the writer can\n     produce: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -I -c 'from datetime import datetime; datetime.fromisoformat(\"2016-12-31T23:59:60+00:00\")'`\n     raises `ValueError: second must be in 0..59` at `c117a695`.\n2. **Resolution function and the `implementer` verb (FR-2).** The env-var and `PATH` evaluation\n   moves out of the hook into the judge. The `--record` write goes through\n   `h_mad_state_write.py`'s write path.\n3. **Claude-side gate on the new state line (FR-3).** This rewrites the hook's state parse and\n3000→   fold, which is where the existing mutation anchors sit (see §\"Risks\"). The same Task applies\n   the AC-3.2 exception (DO-1a): it re-shapes the stub literals in the two named gate test files,\n   updates `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE`, and adds the fixture showing that a\n   today-shape line refuses `judge-error`. The new line must also satisfy the placement\n   invariants P-1..P-5 in DO-1a. Those are assertions on the judge's **real** output, not on stubs,\n   so re-shaping a stub cannot satisfy them. The Task's RED names each one it expects to hold.\n4. **Shared shell-policy module (FR-6).** This is a pure move out of\n   `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`. The destination is `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`,\n   not a new module: D-12, the operator decision on the design's OQ-A (design v1.0 §\"FR-6\"), because a new module the gate\n   imports breaks a hand-built test tree that FR-6 says passes unmodified. Codex-gate behaviour does not change. Every moved name\n3010→   that an existing test reaches stays bound on the Codex gate module, by import.\n   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command` at 2 sites\n   (`grep -n '_safe_shell_command' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` → 2 matching\n   lines at `e7730f78`). The mutation rows in the moved code (M-4) are re-pointed to the new\n   module in the same Task. Then each spec that holds one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n5. **agy gate (FR-4), then its wiring check and assembler enforcement (FR-5).** The gate imports\n   the module from stage 4 and the judge from stage 2. **Exit codes follow the audit-gate signal\n   discipline** (`h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Audit-gate signal discipline\": a normal verdict,\n   PASS or FAIL, prints its token and exits 0; nonzero only for an operational error). Spec v1.4\n   FR-5's exit-code table is the contract, and this plan mirrors it without restating the table:\n3020→   - **Verdicts exit 0.** `AGY-GATE: WIRED` and every `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED reason=…` (all seven\n     reasons, `no-hooks-file` and `self-check-failed` included) exit 0 from the check. The\n     assembler's `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` on `UNWIRED` writes no prompt and\n     exits 0.\n   - **Operational errors exit 2.** `AGY-GATE: UNREADABLE` exits 2 from the check, and the\n     assembler HALT exits 2. A check that prints no `AGY-GATE:` token gives\n     `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token` with exit 2.\n   - The orchestrator halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` in every non-`WIRED` row. Callers\n     read the token, never `$?`.\n   - **Precedent and divergence.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` returns 0 on `WIRING: FAIL` and 2 on\n3030→     `WIRING: UNKNOWN reason=no_settings`\n     (`grep -n 'return [02]' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_hook_wiring.py` at `d90decc6`). The agy check\n     treats a missing `hooks.json` as a verdict instead, deliberately, per spec v1.4 FR-5.\n   - **The assembler then carries two conventions.** Its module docstring says\n     `exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT`\n     (`grep -n '2 on any HALT' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` at `d90decc6`), and its\n     existing HALTs keep exit 2 (spec v1.4 FR-5 residual). The FR-5 Task rewrites that docstring\n     in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, in the same commit, to say which HALT exits which way. A\n     grep-able sentence pins the rewrite, so a stale \"2 on any HALT\" fails a test.","content_concise":"2639→- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n2640→  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n2650→  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n2660→  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n2670→    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n2680→  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n2690→    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n2700→## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n2710→\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n2720→  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n2730→  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n2740→    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n2750→    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n2760→  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n2770→  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n2780→  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n2790→\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n2800→\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n2810→  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n2820→  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n2830→  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n2840→- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\nPaired audited plan: # Plan: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\n2850→Source: `docs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` v1.4, read at commit `e6610381` (v1.2 of\nthis plan; v1.1 read spec v1.3 at `e7730f78`).\nEvery \"currently\" claim below was read from the tree at `17d73037`, and the command that read it is\nwritten beside it. Those readings still hold at `e7730f78` for every `h-mad/` file:\n`git diff --stat 17d73037 e7730f78 -- h-mad` prints nothing. Claims first added in v1.1 are stamped\n`e7730f78`. Claims first added in v1.2 are stamped `d90decc6`, and the older readings still hold\nthere for every `h-mad/` file: `git diff --stat 17d73037 d90decc6 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\nv1.5 read spec v1.6 at `c853b207` and design v1.0 at `df46f72a`; its new readings are stamped\n`200ffd52`, and `git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad` prints nothing. v1.6 mirrors spec\nv1.7 at `c117a695` (D-12, D-13, D-14); `git diff --stat 200ffd52 c117a695 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\n2860→Commands run from the repository root unless they start with `cd`.\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nMake the Phase-5 implementer a per-feature choice between two peers, `codex` and `agy`. One\nresolution function in the TDD judge decides who authors. Claude's gate refuses for whichever peer\nis resolved. A new agy write gate enforces test-first on agy's own tools. Every code-review leg\nrecords whether its reviewer was also the implementer, and Phase 7 surfaces that for every\nimplementer, Codex included.\n\n2870→## Overview\n\nToday Codex is the only Phase-5 implementer h-mad accepts. The state schema, Claude's\nauthorship gate, the dispatch assembler and the implementer prompt all hard-code it, and agy has no\nwrite gate at all (spec §\"Problem\"). The operator wants agy usable whenever Codex is unavailable,\nand selectable per feature, with no loss of the test-first guarantee and with any automatic switch\nmade visible. agy is already installed and already dispatched headless by\n`hmad-dispatch exec agy` for review legs. `agy --version` → `1.4.0` at `200ffd52` (it read\n`1.3.6` at `17d73037`). This reading moves with every agy upgrade, so FR-8 and the `SessionStart`\nre-check stamp the version they ran against rather than citing this one.\n2880→\n## Scope\n\nIn scope, all under `h-mad/` unless noted:\n\n- **State**: four optional fields in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` and their rows in\n  `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-1).\n- **Resolution**: one function and one CLI verb, `implementer`, in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`\n  (FR-2).\n- **Claude-side gate**: `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` reads the resolution from the judge and refuses\n2890→  per peer (FR-3).\n- **agy write gate**: a new `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`, with its wiring check (FR-4, FR-5).\n- **Shared shell policy**: one module imported by both the Codex gate and the agy gate (FR-6).\n- **Review independence**: the state record and the Phase-7 warnings (FR-7).\n- **Live tracer**: a real agy RED/GREEN under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, which\n  also sets the `--agent agy` default timeout (FR-8).\n- **Prompt**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` becomes implementer-neutral and gains the\n  WIRE-PIN new-caller rule (FR-9).\n- **Docs**: SKILL.md, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` and `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-10).\n- **Per-project wiring**: a tracked `.agents/hooks.json` in each project that adopts the agy gate\n2900→  (D-2, D-10).\n\nUser-visible behaviour: the operator sets `implementer=codex|agy` on a feature record. The\norchestrator stages whichever peer resolves and prints any switch on the dispatch. A Phase-7 report\nmay now carry `review_non_independent`, `review_independence_unrecorded` or\n`review_independence_malformed` warnings.\n\n## Goals\n\n- **G1 (FR-1).** The schema declares `implementer`, `agy_status`, `implementer_switch` and\n2910→  `review_independence` as optional. Records written before this feature validate unchanged.\n- **G2 (FR-2).** Exactly one function resolves who authors a record. Both the gate and the\n  `implementer` verb read it, and neither re-derives any part of it.\n- **G3 (FR-3).** Claude's gate refuses for the resolved peer (`codex-authorship` /\n  `agy-authorship`). It refuses `implementer-invalid` first. The `fallback_agent` fold fires only\n  when both peers are out. The three existing message texts stay byte-identical.\n- **G4 (FR-4, FR-6).** agy cannot write a production `.py` without a failing test during active\n  step5, through any tool. Tools the gate does not know are denied, and the shell gets Codex's\n  command-shape rule from the same implementation.\n- **G5 (FR-5).** A project that lacks the wiring halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` before any\n2920→  agy prompt is written. It never passes silently.\n- **G6 (FR-7; D-9, D-11).** Every code-review leg records whether it was independent, for **every**\n  implementer (`codex|agy|grok|claude`). Phase 7 warns on a non-independent leg, on a malformed\n  entry, and — whoever implemented — on a 6a-prime leg that ran with no `leg=6a-prime` entry. These\n  are warnings only, never blockers.\n- **G7 (FR-8).** A real refusal by agy is observed, a real agy RED/GREEN passes the same evidence\n  bar as Codex, and the default timeout is derived from measured turns.\n- **G8 (FR-9, FR-10).** The prompt and the docs stop telling a non-Codex implementer it is Codex,\n  and a valid new-caller RED is no longer halted as `red_wrong_reason`.\n\n2930→## Requirements\n\nThis plan satisfies FR-1 through FR-10 of the spec, with the 19 acceptance criteria AC-1.1, AC-1.2,\nAC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-6.1, AC-7.1, AC-7.2, AC-8.1,\nAC-8.2, AC-9.1, AC-9.2, AC-9.3 and AC-9.4, plus AC-X (`python3 h-mad/scripts/h_mad_ac_census.py\ndocs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` → `ACS: OK count=19 frs=9` at `e8def13f`, spec v1.5, and the same at `c117a695`, spec v1.7). The\ndecisions D-1..D-14 are inputs, not open questions. Spec v1.7 (`c117a695`) adds three, each\nmirrored where it binds:\n\n- **D-12.** The FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not\n2940→  a new module (was the design's OQ-A). Stage 4, DO-5.\n- **D-13.** FR-8 is a post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded\n  COMPLETE only after it passes, with AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 (was the design's OQ-B). Stage 8, DO-6, R-2.\n- **D-14.** `implementer_switch.ts` is narrowed to seconds `00`–`59`; a leap second is refused on\n  both validator paths, because h-mad's writer produces `.ts` through Python `datetime`, which\n  cannot hold second 60. Stage 1.\n\nTwo of the earlier decisions must be carried exactly as the spec states them, because the\nbrainstorm's framing was narrower:\n\n2950→- **D-9.** The non-independent flag fires whenever `reviewer == implementer`, Codex reviewing Codex\n  included (for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex on a Codex feature).\n- **D-11.** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires for **every** implementer. Its trigger is that\n  a 6a-prime leg ran (`archreview` is `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` or `NO`) and there is no\n  `leg=6a-prime` entry. No grandfather rule applies. No surface this feature writes may restate\n  that trigger as agy-only.\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n**Order of work.** Each stage depends only on the stages before it. Each stage is one or more\n2960→impl-plan Tasks, and the full h-mad suite runs per Task (see §\"Success Criteria\").\n\n1. **Schema and writer (FR-1).** Declare the four fields. Also rewrite the `fallback_agent`\n   `description` in `h_mad_state_schema.json` and its row in `state-schema.md`. Both currently tie\n   the field to Codex alone: the schema says it \"is read only when codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n 'read only when codex is out' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`), and the\n   reference row says \"Optional Phase-5 author when Codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n '^| .fallback_agent' h-mad/references/state-schema.md`). After FR-3 both are false:\n   the field is read only when **both** peers are out. Spec v1.3 FR-1 now lists both surfaces.\n   The schema description is pinned:\n2970→   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\n   asserts four substrings, `read only when codex is out`, `are equivalent`,\n   `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE does not override` and `prose-enforced`\n   (`grep -n -A 14 'def test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py`\n   at `e7730f78`). The rewrite keeps all four, or changes that test to the new fact in the same\n   commit. The test is never deleted. The Task's RED names which of the two it does.\n   - **The `codex_status` description and row are rewritten too (spec v1.7 FR-1, AC-1.1), on\n     both surfaces.** The schema `description` says `exhausted`/`unavailable` records that Codex\n     cannot author, \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\", and the `state-schema.md` row says\n     \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\" (at `c117a695`,\n2980→     `grep -c 'permitting Claude to write the fallback' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` → 1\n     line and `grep -c 'permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback' h-mad/references/state-schema.md` → 1\n     line). Both are false once agy is available. The rewrite states authorship as FR-2's\n     resolution, Claude only when `resolved` = `claude`, and leaves the audit-routing half\n     unchanged. The Task's RED pins one sentence on each surface. No test pins the old text today:\n     `git ls-files -z h-mad/tests | xargs -0 grep -l 'permitting Claude to write the fallback\\|permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\\|One switch, both effects\\|blocked when Codex is available' | wc -l`\n     → 0 files at `c117a695`. That zero is load-bearing only for this rewrite, so the Task re-runs\n     the grep. `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` pins property ORDER\n     (`fallback_agent` directly after `codex_status`), so the FR-1 fields are never inserted\n     between the two.\n2990→   - **AC-1.2's leap-second fixture (D-14).** `implementer_switch.ts` = `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` is\n     its own fixture, refused on the library path and, under the existing seam that forces the\n     bundled validator, on the bundled path, one run per path. The writer's form\n     `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both. The narrowing refuses nothing the writer can\n     produce: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -I -c 'from datetime import datetime; datetime.fromisoformat(\"2016-12-31T23:59:60+00:00\")'`\n     raises `ValueError: second must be in 0..59` at `c117a695`.\n2. **Resolution function and the `implementer` verb (FR-2).** The env-var and `PATH` evaluation\n   moves out of the hook into the judge. The `--record` write goes through\n   `h_mad_state_write.py`'s write path.\n3. **Claude-side gate on the new state line (FR-3).** This rewrites the hook's state parse and\n3000→   fold, which is where the existing mutation anchors sit (see §\"Risks\"). The same Task applies\n   the AC-3.2 exception (DO-1a): it re-shapes the stub literals in the two named gate test files,\n   updates `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE`, and adds the fixture showing that a\n   today-shape line refuses `judge-error`. The new line must also satisfy the placement\n   invariants P-1..P-5 in DO-1a. Those are assertions on the judge's **real** output, not on stubs,\n   so re-shaping a stub cannot satisfy them. The Task's RED names each one it expects to hold.\n4. **Shared shell-policy module (FR-6).** This is a pure move out of\n   `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`. The destination is `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`,\n   not a new module: D-12, the operator decision on the design's OQ-A (design v1.0 §\"FR-6\"), because a new module the gate\n   imports breaks a hand-built test tree that FR-6 says passes unmodified. Codex-gate behaviour does not change. Every moved name\n3010→   that an existing test reaches stays bound on the Codex gate module, by import.\n   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command` at 2 sites\n   (`grep -n '_safe_shell_command' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` → 2 matching\n   lines at `e7730f78`). The mutation rows in the moved code (M-4) are re-pointed to the new\n   module in the same Task. Then each spec that holds one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n5. **agy gate (FR-4), then its wiring check and assembler enforcement (FR-5).** The gate imports\n   the module from stage 4 and the judge from stage 2. **Exit codes follow the audit-gate signal\n   discipline** (`h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Audit-gate signal discipline\": a normal verdict,\n   PASS or FAIL, prints its token and exits 0; nonzero only for an operational error). Spec v1.4\n   FR-5's exit-code table is the contract, and this plan mirrors it without restating the table:\n3020→   - **Verdicts exit 0.** `AGY-GATE: WIRED` and every `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED reason=…` (all seven\n     reasons, `no-hooks-file` and `self-check-failed` included) exit 0 from the check. The\n     assembler's `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` on `UNWIRED` writes no prompt and\n     exits 0.\n   - **Operational errors exit 2.** `AGY-GATE: UNREADABLE` exits 2 from the check, and the\n     assembler HALT exits 2. A check that prints no `AGY-GATE:` token gives\n     `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token` with exit 2.\n   - The orchestrator halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` in every non-`WIRED` row. Callers\n     read the token, never `$?`.\n   - **Precedent and divergence.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` returns 0 on `WIRING: FAIL` and 2 on\n3030→     `WIRING: UNKNOWN reason=no_settings`\n     (`grep -n 'return [02]' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_hook_wiring.py` at `d90decc6`). The agy check\n     treats a missing `hooks.json` as a verdict instead, deliberately, per spec v1.4 FR-5.\n   - **The assembler then carries two conventions.** Its module docstring says\n     `exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT`\n     (`grep -n '2 on any HALT' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` at `d90decc6`), and its\n     existing HALTs keep exit 2 (spec v1.4 FR-5 residual). The FR-5 Task rewrites that docstring\n     in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, in the same commit, to say which HALT exits which way. A\n     grep-able sentence pins the rewrite, so a stale \"2 on any HALT\" fails a test.","absolute_path":"/Users/kimhawk/.grok/sessions/%2FUsers%2Fkimhawk%2F.local%2Fstate%2Fh-mad%2Fgrok-audit-surface%2Frun-ab2c85dc2e25-20261024T132452Z%2Froots%2Fgas-root-d8-s2v1-isogl9ls/01a16e83-9639-7733-9916-502222a5a3fb/prompts/prompt_0.txt","offset":2639,"limit":400,"raw_output":"- **AC-8.2:** The probe directory holds the forced-handler-timeout reading from FR-8 step 2a,\n  stamped with the sha, `agy --version` and the T it used. The reading has three parts: whether\n  the file exists, the wall time and agy's message. It names exactly one outcome from step 2a's\n  table, and that outcome is consistent with the three readings. It also shows that the delay\n  hook was removed and that `.agents/hooks.json` matches its pre-probe bytes. A `session-error`\n  outcome does not satisfy this AC. If the outcome is `timeout-allows`, `agy-runtime.md` §\"The TDD\n  gate\" carries the fail-open residual, and the operator question is raised before the gate is\n  called verified.\n- **AC-9.1:** The prompt contains no implementer-specific opening. `test_prompt_file_is_agent_independent`\n  covers `codex`, `grok` and `agy`. The new-caller rule appears in RED item 1, in the WIRE-PIN\n  paragraph, in the GREEN variant, in the SKILL.md 5d halt text, in the `failure-recovery.md`\n  `step5d:red_wrong_reason:<module>` row and in the wiring-task header that `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`\n  emits. `test_h_mad_wiring_task_shape_docs.py`, `test_h_mad_agent_definitions.py` and\n  `test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` stay green. FR-9's sweep, re-run on the shipped tree, hits no file\n  that states the rule without the carve-out. The sweep is the union FR-9 defines: the line grep\n  over every tracked file and the joined reading, which takes in `.py` comment text. The re-run\n  reports `unparsed=N`. A nonzero `N` does not satisfy this AC until each unparsed file has been\n  read by hand.\n- **AC-9.2:** The FR-9 differential corpus runs all four cases (`new-caller`, `missing-callee`,\n  `both-missing`, `unrelated-import`) as fixture repositories with real pytest REDs. For each case\n  it records the RED's exception line, whether the callee resolves at the RED base (by import) and\n  both verdicts. The set of cases whose verdict changed is exactly `{new-caller}`.\n- **AC-9.3:** The FR-9 incident replay covers HemaSuite `notebooklm-integration-interface` T4\n  WIRE-PIN 1 (`test_notebooklm_resolver.py` at `531a6296b`), T5 WIRE-PIN 1\n  (`test_notebooklm_initialize.py` at `7aa0d9dec`) and T6 WIRE-PIN 2\n  (`test_notebooklm_status_report.py` at `c2d671f6b`). For each case the committed probe holds:\n  - the direct-call form's RED reproduced at the task commit's parent: `AttributeError`/\n    `ImportError` on the caller, with the callee resolving by import. For T4 this is the committed\n    pin; for T5 and T6 it is the pin with the scaffold replaced, per FR-9;\n  - on that RED, the new rule's verdict **valid** and the old rule's verdict halt;\n  - for T5 and T6 only, the committed scaffolded pin's RED (`AssertionError`) and the old rule's\n    verdict on it, which is valid;\n  - a wire-cut re-run at the task commit in which the committed pin fails;\n  - a citation of the original evidence (M4.1, M5.1, WIRE 2 revert).\n\n  It is stamped with both repositories' shas. A case that cannot be reproduced is recorded with\n  its failing command and counted. It does not satisfy the AC.\n- **AC-9.4:** The six copies of the new-caller rule (FR-9 surfaces 1–5 and the GREEN variant) are\n  bound to one authority. Either every surface is generated from or checked against one shared\n  source, or one test asserts that the carve-out text is equal across all six copies. The plan\n  names which mechanism. Whichever it is:\n  - **The carve-out text is the whole quoted block in FR-9**, all three sentences: the validity\n    condition, \"The RED report must name both.\", and the wire-cut obligation. A check that binds\n    only the first sentence does not meet this AC.\n  - **Each copy is read from its own source location.** The span read for one copy contains no\n    other copy. Three readings do not meet this AC, because a healthy copy in the same span\n    masks a sick one: a contains-check over the whole assembled prompt (the template's copies\n    1, 2 and 6 are filled into it beside the header copy); a file-level contains-check over\n    `codex-implementer-prompt.md`, which holds three copies; and a file-level check over any\n    file that holds more than one copy. The design names each copy's locator. A locator states\n    what it does not pin, and the test fails, rather than passing, when a locator matches\n    nothing or more than one span.\n  - **The control is a mutation of exactly one copy, and it makes the check fail.** It is run\n    once per copy, so six times, and once per sentence of the block in at least one copy. Each\n    mutation row's `find` is unique in its file, because `h_mad_mutation_harness.py`\n    `anchor_status` refuses a `find` that does not occur exactly once. A `find` therefore carries\n    copy-specific context, and the `.py` copy's `find` is written against the source bytes,\n    including the adjacent-literal split. A row the harness refuses is not a control.\n- **AC-X:** Every new guard carries a mutation row that bites (`MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT` with a `test`\n  per row). The full suite passes per task.\n\n## Decisions on the brainstorm's open questions\n\n- **D-1 `run_command`:** command-shape rule shared with Codex (FR-6). Rationale: the tree shows\n  that is what Codex parity is.\n- **D-2 Hook scope:** per project (`.agents/hooks.json`). Rationale:\n  - The Codex gate is wired per project (HemaSuite `.codex/hooks.json`; this repository has no\n    `.codex/`).\n  - A tracked file reaches every worktree.\n  - The handler cwd gives the root without trusting a payload field.\n  - No global hook fires in every agy session on the machine.\n\n  Residual: a project without the file has no agy gate. FR-5 turns that into a halt, not a silent\n  pass.\n- **D-3 Payload cwd:** the root comes from the handler cwd (FR-4), with `workspacePaths` as a\n  cross-check.\n- **D-4 Timeout:** provisional 1500 s, then FR-8's formula. The hook-handler timeout is a separate\n  figure, derived from `JUDGE_BUDGET_S`.\n- **D-5 Availability:** CLI on PATH + `agy_status` + `HMAD_AGY_UNAVAILABLE`, symmetric with Codex.\n  No liveness probe: the gate runs on every write and must be offline and fast, and a probe spends\n  quota. An exhausted agy shows up when the dispatch fails, and the orchestrator records it with\n  `--set agy_status=exhausted`.\n- **D-6 Project default:** feature record only. A second source doubles the surface the gate\n  resolves, and per-feature is the operator decision. A later default can seed the same field at\n  `--create`.\n- **D-7 Flag location:** a state field (FR-7). It is not on the extracted verdict, because that\n  line is `<KEY>: <VALUE>` and its consumers parse it.\n- **D-8 Audit legs:** unchanged (FR-10).\n- **D-9 Codex reviewing Codex (was OQ-1; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** yes. FR-7's flag fires\n  for any `reviewer == implementer`, Codex included, for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier\n  dispatched to Codex on a Codex-implemented feature. It is a warning only, never a block.\n  Consequence: Codex-implemented features can now carry a `review_non_independent` Phase-7\n  warning that has never appeared before.\n- **D-10 `.agents/` exclusion (was OQ-2; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** narrow it. Applied in this\n  clone by the orchestrator on the operator's answer: the `.git/info/exclude` entry `/.agents/` is now `/.agents/skills/`.\n  Evidence at `d65374e9`: `git check-ignore -v .agents/hooks.json` prints nothing (exit 1, not\n  ignored), and `git check-ignore -v .agents/skills` still reports the `/.agents/skills/` entry.\n  D-2's tracked `.agents/hooks.json` therefore needs no `git add -f`.\n  - **Residual / prerequisite.** `.git/info/exclude` is per-clone local configuration and is not\n    versioned. Every other clone of this repository, and every other project that adopts the agy\n    gate, needs the same narrowing (or a `git add -f`) before its `.agents/hooks.json` can be\n    tracked. Linked worktrees share the main clone's `info/exclude` (git's common dir), so they\n    inherit the narrowing from the clone they belong to. Where the file is not tracked, FR-5's\n    check reads `UNWIRED reason=no-hooks-file` in any worktree that lacks it, so the gap is a halt,\n    not a silent pass.\n- **D-11 `review_independence_unrecorded` for every implementer (operator decision, 2026-10-16):**\n  widened from agy-only to `codex|agy|grok|claude`, matching D-9 (FR-7). Warning only, never a\n  block. Rationale: it fires only when the recording step was skipped, so a feature that follows\n  the new flow stays quiet; without it, a missing record would hide a Codex-on-Codex self-review,\n  which D-9 now flags when it IS recorded.\n  - **Pre-existing features — settled, no grandfather rule.** The check applies to every feature\n    whose Phase-7 check runs after this ships. Archived features are untouched because nothing\n    re-runs the check over them (FR-7). A grandfather rule would need a signal separating \"began\n    before this shipped\" from \"skipped the recording step\", and the only signal the record offers\n    — absence of `review_independence` — is the skip itself, so any such rule would also silence\n    the case the warning exists for. A warning on an in-flight feature costs one backfilled entry\n    or one report line.\n- **D-12 Shared shell-policy module (was the design's OQ-A; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** the\n  FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not a new module.\n  The design's reason: `test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` `test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason`\n  builds a tree by copying only the Codex gate, a judge stub and `h_mad_target_identity.py`\n  (`grep -n -A12 'def test_codex_gate_judge_timeout_reason' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py`),\n  so a new module would be missing there, and FR-6 forbids editing that test.\n- **D-13 FR-8 timing (was the design's OQ-B; operator decision, 2026-10-16):** FR-8 runs as a\n  post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded COMPLETE only after it.\n  Reason: the tracer must run the gate at its installed path, which is main.\n- **D-14 Leap-second `.ts` refused (orchestrator decision, design audit cycle 1, 2026-10-16):**\n  FR-1 narrows `implementer_switch.ts` to seconds `00`–`59`. Reason: state timestamps are written\n  by h-mad's own writer through Python `datetime`, which cannot produce a seconds field of `60`.\n- **D-15 Durable author record (OD-E2, operator decision, 2026-10-16: option (a)):** FR-1 gains\n  `implementation_authors`, an optional array of `{module, agent}` entries appended (never\n  removed or altered) through FR-7's Record mechanism, one per accepted 5e GREEN or accepted fix\n  dispatch and never for a 5d RED. FR-7 §\"Attribution\" reads a module's author set from it.\n  Reason: design v1.2 (OD-E2) showed AC-7.3's \"multi-author, recovered\" fixture could not pass\n  without a durable author record, because the assembler's prompt, out and log files are under\n  `/tmp` and its slug names no agent. The fixture stays as written and now has its source. The\n  rejected option (b) was to amend the fixture to \"no entry\".\n- **D-16 Directory target refused under active step5 (OD-2, operator decision, 2026-10-16):** a\n  class-(b) target that names an existing directory is refused with `kind=judge-error` when the\n  scan reads `active` or `unknown`, and allowed when it reads `inactive` (FR-4 deliberate\n  difference 6). Reason: impl-plan v1.1 Task 6 showed that `canonicalise` returns no name and\n  no `unresolvable` flag for `.`, `src/`, `src` and the root, so the class-(b) sequence would\n  otherwise allow a directory target under `active`. Residual: a trailing-`/` path that names no\n  existing directory is not covered and is judged as a file name.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\nNone — OQ-1/OQ-2 settled 2026-10-16; the design's OQ-A/OQ-B settled 2026-10-16 (D-12, D-13);\nthe design's OD-E2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-15); the impl-plan's OD-2 settled 2026-10-16 (D-16).\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- Per-task implementer selection. It can come later on the same field.\n- Refusing a peer's production write in that peer's own gate when the other peer is the resolved\n  implementer, for example a Codex verifier dispatch writing production code under\n  `implementer=agy`. Residual: the test-first judge still applies, and the per-dispatch\n  `git status --short` diff (agy-runtime.md §\"Author and reviewer roles\") is what shows it.\n- A write-time gate for grok, and authorship rules for agy as the orchestrating host.\n- Routing audit legs by `agy_status`.\n\n## Owed by other documents\n\nNone of these are edited by this spec.\n- `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` says there is no `SessionStart` on two surfaces:\n  - §\"Hooks\" (\"There is **no `SessionStart`**\");\n  - the §\"Construct mapping\" table's `hook-event` row (\"there is no `SessionStart`\").\n\n  agy's current hooks.md lists `SessionStart` as a supported event. Both surfaces need a re-check\n  against the installed agy (`agy --version` prints 1.4.0 at `200ffd52`), they are corrected\n  together, and the correction stamps the version it was checked against.\n  (`grep -n 'SessionStart' h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` at `200ffd52`: 2 matching lines, one\n  under each heading.) Residual: this is a content predicate, so a third restatement added later\n  is found only by re-running the grep.\n- **Design (v1.8 items).** FR-4: enumerate the closed kind list and give every deny path a member\n  (E6); its claim that `unknown` \"denies `kind=judge-error`, as the Codex gate does for a shell\n  command\" is false at `cff71b49` — the Codex gate's `unknown` shell deny carries no `kind=`\n  token. FR-7: name where the complete author set is kept (E2) and correct \"the GREEN dispatch the\n  orchestrator accepted at 5d\" to 5e (E4).\n- **Design (v1.9, D-15).** FR-1: add `implementation_authors` with FR-1's entry schema to the\n  schema JSON and a `state-schema.md` row. FR-7: retire \"the complete set is kept in the\n  procedure\" and OD-E2 in favour of reading the set from `implementation_authors`; the\n  `--append` mechanism (DO-3) must accept this key with the same guarantees, and the \"When the\n  dispatch list is lost\" rule becomes FR-7's three closed unrecoverable cases.\n- **Design (spec v1.10).** FR-4: record D-16 as a directory check before the target is judged,\n  and add it to §\"Deliberate differences\" as item 6; FR-4 `--self-check`: a verdict `FAIL` exits\n  0 (replacing \"a nonzero exit\"), and the Codex gate's `FAIL shell-policy` is corrected in impl-plan\n  Task 4; FR-2: adopt the halts `step5:implementer_invalid` and `step5:implementer_unresolved`.\n- **Impl-plan.** AC-7.3's surface (FR-7 §\"Attribution\" must have code to drive, or the AC is an\n  OPEN-DECISION), now including the pre-field fixture; AC-1.3's fixtures; AC-4.1's `unknown`\n  fixtures and AC-4.2's per-deny-path kind fixtures.\n- **Plan.** If the plan carries FR-1's field list or AC-7.3's fixtures, it gains\n  `implementation_authors`, AC-1.3 and the pre-field fixture.\n\n## Version History\n- v1.0: Initial spec (2026-10-16) from brainstorm v1.1. Settles 8 open questions (D-1..D-8): run_command takes the Codex command-shape rule, not a stated residual; per-project .agents/hooks.json; root from handler cwd; provisional 1500 s timeout then tracer formula; PATH+status+env availability; feature-only implementer; flag in state; audit legs unchanged. Folds brief fix 3 (#8) WIRE-PIN new-caller RED case as FR-9. OQ-1 (Codex-reviews-Codex flag), OQ-2 (.agents exclude) for the operator.\n- v1.1: Operator decisions 2026-10-16 (2026-10-16). OQ-1 -> D-9: FR-7 flag fires for any reviewer == implementer, Codex reviewing Codex included; warning only. OQ-2 -> D-10: operator narrowed .git/info/exclude /.agents/ to /.agents/skills/ (check-ignore evidence at d65374e9), no git add -f; per-clone residual stated. Open questions section now empty.\n- v1.2: Operator decision 2026-10-16 -> D-11: FR-7 review_independence_unrecorded widened from agy-only to every implementer (codex|agy|grok|claude), matching D-9; warning only. Trigger pinned to a 6a-prime leg that ran (archreview READY_TO_MERGE|WITH_FIXES|NO) with no leg=6a-prime entry; other archreview values already surface. Pre-existing features settled: no grandfather rule, archived features untouched (no batch caller). AC-7.2 added.\n- v1.3: Plan-author findings against v1.2 (2026-10-16). FR-3: a missing state-line field refuses judge-error (tree precedent: missing-field fixture), so the two gate stub-line fixtures are re-shaped with assertions byte-identical — the AC-3.2 exception; test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py ACTIVE_RE and line-quoting mutation rows follow the line. FR-7: writer has no append (--set replaces); add-without-loss is a requirement, mechanism a design obligation; AC-7.1 gains the two-entry fixture. FR-6: rows re-pointed to the shared module, then ALL_CAUGHT; moved names stay bound on the Codex gate module. FR-9/AC-9.1/FR-10: failure-recovery.md red_wrong_reason row; rule surfaces closed with sweep. FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: fallback_agent description on schema JSON and state-schema.md, pinned literals named. Owed: second SessionStart surface (Construct-mapping hook-event row). All ACs reformatted to the h_mad_ac_census.py grammar (- **AC-n.m:**).\n- v1.4: Plan audit cycle 1 findings owned by the spec (2026-10-16). F1 -> FR-5 exit-code table: AGY-GATE WIRED/UNWIRED (all seven reasons, no-hooks-file included) exit 0 and the assembler's agy_tdd_hook_unverified verdict HALT exits 0; UNREADABLE and a no-token check exit 2; existing assembler HALTs keep exit 2 (residual); AC-5.2. F2 -> FR-1: .ts is RFC 3339 date-time; neither validator path enforces format today (probed), so refusal on both paths is the requirement and the mechanism a design obligation; rfc3339-validator hazard; residual for started_ts/owner_heartbeat_ts/halt_ts; AC-1.2. F3 -> FR-9 differential corpus over four missing-symbol cases, changed set == {new-caller}; AC-9.2. F4 -> FR-9 incident replay of HemaSuite notebooklm F-3 (T4/T5 WIRE-PIN 1, T6 WIRE-PIN 2) with wire-cut re-run; AC-9.3. F5 -> FR-8 step 2a forced handler timeout with four classified outcomes; step 2 now settles two unknowns; FR-5 re-run trigger includes it; AC-8.2. F6 -> no contradiction; FR-3 now lists the real-line assertions (two in test_h_mad_tdd_gate_fallback_agent.py, four tests in test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py) as placement constraints; AC-3.2 extended.\n- v1.5: Plan audit cycle 2 findings owned by the spec, final corrective revision after the round cap (2026-10-16). B -> FR-10 adds the SKILL.md teammate-audit bullet: audit-leg selection vs resolved authorship (Claude only when resolved=claude), anchor prefix and the four pinned tokens kept. C -> FR-9 surface census gains surface 5, the h_mad_assemble_tdd.py assemble() wiring header (split string literals), and its pin test_h_mad_assemble_tdd.py TestWiringShape.test_the_pin_is_told_what_its_red_must_be; the census method now joins Python adjacent literals via ast and collapses markdown whitespace, measured at 9c3b799d; residual: run-time-built strings. AC-9.1 lists the header. D -> FR-9 replay and AC-9.3 name all three pins with files and commits (531a6296b, 7aa0d9dec, c2d671f6b); T5/T6 replay evaluates the direct-call / two-argument getattr form; the committed scaffolded RED (AssertionError) is recorded as old-verdict valid, correcting the false old-verdict=halt claim. A -> new AC-9.4: the six rule copies bound to one authority, with a per-copy control.\n- v1.6: Advisory delta review of plan v1.3 (608e430), spec-owned parts (2026-10-16). Should-fix 3 -> FR-9: the joined reading (ast str constants) is blind to non-str text in a .py file; axis named with three members (comments, files ast.parse rejects, bytes literals). The sweep is now the union of the line grep and the joined reading, which adds joined .py comment tokens; parse failures are reported as unparsed=N and read by hand; residual: run-time-built strings and a bytes literal split across lines. Measured at 5f72c2f3: union 7 files, line-grep-minus-joined 0, comment hits 0 (incidental), unparsed=0. AC-9.1 re-run is the union and reports unparsed=N. Must-fix 1 (spec side) and Should-fix 4 -> AC-9.4: the carve-out text is the whole three-sentence FR-9 block; each copy is read from its own source location, never from the assembled prompt or a file-level contains-check over a file holding more than one copy; a locator matching zero or several spans fails the test; the control mutates exactly one copy, runs once per copy and once per sentence in at least one copy, with a find unique in its file (mutation harness anchor_status), the .py find written against source bytes including the literal split.\n- v1.7: Design audit cycle 1, spec-owned items (2026-10-16). S1 (codex C1) -> FR-1/AC-1.1/AC-1.2 + D-14: .ts narrowed to seconds 00-59, a leap second (2016-12-31T23:59:60Z) refused on both paths; reason: every .ts comes from Python datetime via the writer, which cannot hold second 60 (orchestrator decision). S2 (codex C6) -> FR-7 Attribution: an entry's implementer is the author of the reviewed code, from its successful implementation dispatch, never implementer_switch.to (historical) or the implementer field; multi-author code is non-independent if the reviewer is any author. S3 -> FR-1/FR-10/AC-1.1: codex_status schema description and state-schema.md row rewritten to FR-2 authorship (no test pins the text; the fallback_agent-after-codex_status order pin stated); FR-8 Version: 1.4.0 at 200ffd52, each reading stamps its version; Owed SessionStart re-check re-stamped 1.4.0 at 200ffd52; FR-6/AC-6.1 shared module = h_mad_target_identity.py (D-12, OQ-A); FR-8/AC-8.1 post-merge stage, COMPLETE only after it (D-13, OQ-B). Open questions: OQ-A/OQ-B settled.\n- v1.8: Design audit cycle 2, final corrective revision after the round cap, not re-audited (2026-10-16). S1/E6 -> FR-4 'Every deny carries a kind': rule over every deny path, closed list owned by the design, spec names only judge-error, tool-not-admitted and the judge's Verdict kind; Codex gate has 6 of 13 kindless _deny call sites at cff71b49. E1 -> FR-4 'The activity reading': three-valued scan (active/unknown/inactive), every step5-gated deny also fires on unknown, class-(b) production .py still read through read_chain under inactive; workspacePaths and Tool classes sentences re-worded. E5 -> FR-4 Target keys: a non-empty-string value counts as no path key. S4 -> FR-4 'Deliberate differences from the Codex gate': design's four adopted plus the kind rule as a fifth. S2/E2/E4 -> FR-7 Attribution sub-bullets (5e GREEN + accepted fixes, never 5d RED; complete author set, never a projection) and new AC-7.3. S5 -> adopted: reviewer none of the authors names the latest accepted dispatch's author. S3 -> leap-second ValueError quote drops the version-dependent tail. AC-4.1/AC-4.2 extended; FR-6 states the unknown-scan shell deny; Owed gains design and impl-plan items.\n- v1.9: Operator decision OD-E2 (a) (2026-10-16) -> D-15: FR-1 gains implementation_authors, an optional array of {module, agent} (agent codex|agy|grok|claude; module non-empty string; no extra keys), appended never altered through FR-7's Record mechanism on accepting a 5e GREEN or fix dispatch, never 5d RED or a halted/timed-out/rejected dispatch; duplicates kept, array order = acceptance order. New AC-1.3 (valid entries accepted, unknown agent and other malformed shapes refused, both validator paths). FR-7 Attribution reads the author set from the field on every leg; unrecoverable cases closed to three (field absent: pre-field features; no entry for the module: a record-step defect; malformed) -> no entry. AC-7.3 multi-author fixture unchanged with its source named; pre-field fixture added. AC-7.2 'only FR-1 field' narrowed to routing field. FR-10 SKILL.md Phase-5 step bullet. Owed: design FR-1/FR-7 (retire procedure-kept set and OD-E2), impl-plan AC-1.3 + pre-field fixture, plan mirror.\n- v1.10: Owed lines from impl-plan v1.1 (Phase 5b), final corrective revision, not re-audited (2026-10-16). P1 -> D-16 (OD-2, operator): a class-(b) target naming an existing directory denies kind=judge-error under active/unknown, allowed under inactive; FR-4 deliberate difference 6, AC-4.1 fixture; residual: trailing-/ path naming no existing directory. P2 -> FR-2 halts named: step5:implementer_invalid (invalid), step5:implementer_unresolved (no-record, unreadable), step5:implementer_switch_unrecorded (record=failed); adopted from impl-plan Task 16; failure-recovery.md rows not added (residual). P3 -> FR-5: the gate's --self-check verdict FAIL prints its token on stdout and exits 0, operational error nonzero; Codex gate FAIL shell-policy corrected in impl-plan Task 4; Codex FAIL parser residual; AC-5.2 gains the self-check fixture and mutant. P4 -> FR-9 Hook line keeps Claude's h-mad-tdd-gate.sh (test_hook_line_names_both_gates). Owed: design FR-4/FR-2 lines.\n\nPaired audited plan: # Plan: agy-coder-fallback\n\n> **Slug note.** The slug predates brainstorm v1.1. agy is a **peer** Phase-5 implementer with\n> Codex's standing, not a fallback (spec §\"Slug note\").\n\nSource: `docs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` v1.4, read at commit `e6610381` (v1.2 of\nthis plan; v1.1 read spec v1.3 at `e7730f78`).\nEvery \"currently\" claim below was read from the tree at `17d73037`, and the command that read it is\nwritten beside it. Those readings still hold at `e7730f78` for every `h-mad/` file:\n`git diff --stat 17d73037 e7730f78 -- h-mad` prints nothing. Claims first added in v1.1 are stamped\n`e7730f78`. Claims first added in v1.2 are stamped `d90decc6`, and the older readings still hold\nthere for every `h-mad/` file: `git diff --stat 17d73037 d90decc6 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\nv1.5 read spec v1.6 at `c853b207` and design v1.0 at `df46f72a`; its new readings are stamped\n`200ffd52`, and `git diff --stat 29ae3e7b 200ffd52 -- h-mad` prints nothing. v1.6 mirrors spec\nv1.7 at `c117a695` (D-12, D-13, D-14); `git diff --stat 200ffd52 c117a695 -- h-mad` prints nothing.\nCommands run from the repository root unless they start with `cd`.\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nMake the Phase-5 implementer a per-feature choice between two peers, `codex` and `agy`. One\nresolution function in the TDD judge decides who authors. Claude's gate refuses for whichever peer\nis resolved. A new agy write gate enforces test-first on agy's own tools. Every code-review leg\nrecords whether its reviewer was also the implementer, and Phase 7 surfaces that for every\nimplementer, Codex included.\n\n## Overview\n\nToday Codex is the only Phase-5 implementer h-mad accepts. The state schema, Claude's\nauthorship gate, the dispatch assembler and the implementer prompt all hard-code it, and agy has no\nwrite gate at all (spec §\"Problem\"). The operator wants agy usable whenever Codex is unavailable,\nand selectable per feature, with no loss of the test-first guarantee and with any automatic switch\nmade visible. agy is already installed and already dispatched headless by\n`hmad-dispatch exec agy` for review legs. `agy --version` → `1.4.0` at `200ffd52` (it read\n`1.3.6` at `17d73037`). This reading moves with every agy upgrade, so FR-8 and the `SessionStart`\nre-check stamp the version they ran against rather than citing this one.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope, all under `h-mad/` unless noted:\n\n- **State**: four optional fields in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` and their rows in\n  `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-1).\n- **Resolution**: one function and one CLI verb, `implementer`, in `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_tdd_judge.py`\n  (FR-2).\n- **Claude-side gate**: `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-tdd-gate.sh` reads the resolution from the judge and refuses\n  per peer (FR-3).\n- **agy write gate**: a new `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-agy-tdd-gate.py`, with its wiring check (FR-4, FR-5).\n- **Shared shell policy**: one module imported by both the Codex gate and the agy gate (FR-6).\n- **Review independence**: the state record and the Phase-7 warnings (FR-7).\n- **Live tracer**: a real agy RED/GREEN under `docs/03-analysis/probes/agy-coder-fallback/`, which\n  also sets the `--agent agy` default timeout (FR-8).\n- **Prompt**: `h-mad/references/codex-implementer-prompt.md` becomes implementer-neutral and gains the\n  WIRE-PIN new-caller rule (FR-9).\n- **Docs**: SKILL.md, `h-mad/references/agy-runtime.md` and `h-mad/references/state-schema.md` (FR-10).\n- **Per-project wiring**: a tracked `.agents/hooks.json` in each project that adopts the agy gate\n  (D-2, D-10).\n\nUser-visible behaviour: the operator sets `implementer=codex|agy` on a feature record. The\norchestrator stages whichever peer resolves and prints any switch on the dispatch. A Phase-7 report\nmay now carry `review_non_independent`, `review_independence_unrecorded` or\n`review_independence_malformed` warnings.\n\n## Goals\n\n- **G1 (FR-1).** The schema declares `implementer`, `agy_status`, `implementer_switch` and\n  `review_independence` as optional. Records written before this feature validate unchanged.\n- **G2 (FR-2).** Exactly one function resolves who authors a record. Both the gate and the\n  `implementer` verb read it, and neither re-derives any part of it.\n- **G3 (FR-3).** Claude's gate refuses for the resolved peer (`codex-authorship` /\n  `agy-authorship`). It refuses `implementer-invalid` first. The `fallback_agent` fold fires only\n  when both peers are out. The three existing message texts stay byte-identical.\n- **G4 (FR-4, FR-6).** agy cannot write a production `.py` without a failing test during active\n  step5, through any tool. Tools the gate does not know are denied, and the shell gets Codex's\n  command-shape rule from the same implementation.\n- **G5 (FR-5).** A project that lacks the wiring halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` before any\n  agy prompt is written. It never passes silently.\n- **G6 (FR-7; D-9, D-11).** Every code-review leg records whether it was independent, for **every**\n  implementer (`codex|agy|grok|claude`). Phase 7 warns on a non-independent leg, on a malformed\n  entry, and — whoever implemented — on a 6a-prime leg that ran with no `leg=6a-prime` entry. These\n  are warnings only, never blockers.\n- **G7 (FR-8).** A real refusal by agy is observed, a real agy RED/GREEN passes the same evidence\n  bar as Codex, and the default timeout is derived from measured turns.\n- **G8 (FR-9, FR-10).** The prompt and the docs stop telling a non-Codex implementer it is Codex,\n  and a valid new-caller RED is no longer halted as `red_wrong_reason`.\n\n## Requirements\n\nThis plan satisfies FR-1 through FR-10 of the spec, with the 19 acceptance criteria AC-1.1, AC-1.2,\nAC-2.1, AC-2.2, AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-6.1, AC-7.1, AC-7.2, AC-8.1,\nAC-8.2, AC-9.1, AC-9.2, AC-9.3 and AC-9.4, plus AC-X (`python3 h-mad/scripts/h_mad_ac_census.py\ndocs/01-plan/features/agy-coder-fallback.spec.md` → `ACS: OK count=19 frs=9` at `e8def13f`, spec v1.5, and the same at `c117a695`, spec v1.7). The\ndecisions D-1..D-14 are inputs, not open questions. Spec v1.7 (`c117a695`) adds three, each\nmirrored where it binds:\n\n- **D-12.** The FR-6 policy moves into the existing `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`, not\n  a new module (was the design's OQ-A). Stage 4, DO-5.\n- **D-13.** FR-8 is a post-merge stage after Phase 7's integrate, and the feature is recorded\n  COMPLETE only after it passes, with AC-8.1 and AC-8.2 (was the design's OQ-B). Stage 8, DO-6, R-2.\n- **D-14.** `implementer_switch.ts` is narrowed to seconds `00`–`59`; a leap second is refused on\n  both validator paths, because h-mad's writer produces `.ts` through Python `datetime`, which\n  cannot hold second 60. Stage 1.\n\nTwo of the earlier decisions must be carried exactly as the spec states them, because the\nbrainstorm's framing was narrower:\n\n- **D-9.** The non-independent flag fires whenever `reviewer == implementer`, Codex reviewing Codex\n  included (for example the 5e anti-gaming verifier dispatched to Codex on a Codex feature).\n- **D-11.** `review_independence_unrecorded` fires for **every** implementer. Its trigger is that\n  a 6a-prime leg ran (`archreview` is `READY_TO_MERGE`, `WITH_FIXES` or `NO`) and there is no\n  `leg=6a-prime` entry. No grandfather rule applies. No surface this feature writes may restate\n  that trigger as agy-only.\n\n## Implementation Strategy\n\n**Order of work.** Each stage depends only on the stages before it. Each stage is one or more\nimpl-plan Tasks, and the full h-mad suite runs per Task (see §\"Success Criteria\").\n\n1. **Schema and writer (FR-1).** Declare the four fields. Also rewrite the `fallback_agent`\n   `description` in `h_mad_state_schema.json` and its row in `state-schema.md`. Both currently tie\n   the field to Codex alone: the schema says it \"is read only when codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n 'read only when codex is out' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json`), and the\n   reference row says \"Optional Phase-5 author when Codex is out\"\n   (`grep -n '^| .fallback_agent' h-mad/references/state-schema.md`). After FR-3 both are false:\n   the field is read only when **both** peers are out. Spec v1.3 FR-1 now lists both surfaces.\n   The schema description is pinned:\n   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py` `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts`\n   asserts four substrings, `read only when codex is out`, `are equivalent`,\n   `HMAD_CODEX_UNAVAILABLE does not override` and `prose-enforced`\n   (`grep -n -A 14 'def test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_state_fallback_agent.py`\n   at `e7730f78`). The rewrite keeps all four, or changes that test to the new fact in the same\n   commit. The test is never deleted. The Task's RED names which of the two it does.\n   - **The `codex_status` description and row are rewritten too (spec v1.7 FR-1, AC-1.1), on\n     both surfaces.** The schema `description` says `exhausted`/`unavailable` records that Codex\n     cannot author, \"permitting Claude to write the fallback\", and the `state-schema.md` row says\n     \"An unavailable or exhausted status permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\" (at `c117a695`,\n     `grep -c 'permitting Claude to write the fallback' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_state_schema.json` → 1\n     line and `grep -c 'permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback' h-mad/references/state-schema.md` → 1\n     line). Both are false once agy is available. The rewrite states authorship as FR-2's\n     resolution, Claude only when `resolved` = `claude`, and leaves the audit-routing half\n     unchanged. The Task's RED pins one sentence on each surface. No test pins the old text today:\n     `git ls-files -z h-mad/tests | xargs -0 grep -l 'permitting Claude to write the fallback\\|permits a Phase-5 authoring fallback\\|One switch, both effects\\|blocked when Codex is available' | wc -l`\n     → 0 files at `c117a695`. That zero is load-bearing only for this rewrite, so the Task re-runs\n     the grep. `test_fallback_agent_description_states_the_four_facts` pins property ORDER\n     (`fallback_agent` directly after `codex_status`), so the FR-1 fields are never inserted\n     between the two.\n   - **AC-1.2's leap-second fixture (D-14).** `implementer_switch.ts` = `2016-12-31T23:59:60Z` is\n     its own fixture, refused on the library path and, under the existing seam that forces the\n     bundled validator, on the bundled path, one run per path. The writer's form\n     `%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ` is accepted on both. The narrowing refuses nothing the writer can\n     produce: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python3 -I -c 'from datetime import datetime; datetime.fromisoformat(\"2016-12-31T23:59:60+00:00\")'`\n     raises `ValueError: second must be in 0..59` at `c117a695`.\n2. **Resolution function and the `implementer` verb (FR-2).** The env-var and `PATH` evaluation\n   moves out of the hook into the judge. The `--record` write goes through\n   `h_mad_state_write.py`'s write path.\n3. **Claude-side gate on the new state line (FR-3).** This rewrites the hook's state parse and\n   fold, which is where the existing mutation anchors sit (see §\"Risks\"). The same Task applies\n   the AC-3.2 exception (DO-1a): it re-shapes the stub literals in the two named gate test files,\n   updates `test_h_mad_tdd_judge.py` `ACTIVE_RE`, and adds the fixture showing that a\n   today-shape line refuses `judge-error`. The new line must also satisfy the placement\n   invariants P-1..P-5 in DO-1a. Those are assertions on the judge's **real** output, not on stubs,\n   so re-shaping a stub cannot satisfy them. The Task's RED names each one it expects to hold.\n4. **Shared shell-policy module (FR-6).** This is a pure move out of\n   `h-mad/hooks/h-mad-codex-tdd-gate.py`. The destination is `h-mad/scripts/h_mad_target_identity.py`,\n   not a new module: D-12, the operator decision on the design's OQ-A (design v1.0 §\"FR-6\"), because a new module the gate\n   imports breaks a hand-built test tree that FR-6 says passes unmodified. Codex-gate behaviour does not change. Every moved name\n   that an existing test reaches stays bound on the Codex gate module, by import.\n   `h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` calls `module._safe_shell_command` at 2 sites\n   (`grep -n '_safe_shell_command' h-mad/tests/test_h_mad_codex_tdd_gate_judge.py` → 2 matching\n   lines at `e7730f78`). The mutation rows in the moved code (M-4) are re-pointed to the new\n   module in the same Task. Then each spec that holds one is gated on `MUTATION: ALL_CAUGHT`.\n5. **agy gate (FR-4), then its wiring check and assembler enforcement (FR-5).** The gate imports\n   the module from stage 4 and the judge from stage 2. **Exit codes follow the audit-gate signal\n   discipline** (`h-mad/invariants.base.md` §\"Audit-gate signal discipline\": a normal verdict,\n   PASS or FAIL, prints its token and exits 0; nonzero only for an operational error). Spec v1.4\n   FR-5's exit-code table is the contract, and this plan mirrors it without restating the table:\n   - **Verdicts exit 0.** `AGY-GATE: WIRED` and every `AGY-GATE: UNWIRED reason=…` (all seven\n     reasons, `no-hooks-file` and `self-check-failed` included) exit 0 from the check. The\n     assembler's `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified` on `UNWIRED` writes no prompt and\n     exits 0.\n   - **Operational errors exit 2.** `AGY-GATE: UNREADABLE` exits 2 from the check, and the\n     assembler HALT exits 2. A check that prints no `AGY-GATE:` token gives\n     `ASSEMBLE-TDD: HALT agy_tdd_hook_unverified check=no-token` with exit 2.\n   - The orchestrator halts `step5:agy_tdd_hook_unverified` in every non-`WIRED` row. Callers\n     read the token, never `$?`.\n   - **Precedent and divergence.** `h_mad_hook_wiring.py` returns 0 on `WIRING: FAIL` and 2 on\n     `WIRING: UNKNOWN reason=no_settings`\n     (`grep -n 'return [02]' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_hook_wiring.py` at `d90decc6`). The agy check\n     treats a missing `hooks.json` as a verdict instead, deliberately, per spec v1.4 FR-5.\n   - **The assembler then carries two conventions.** Its module docstring says\n     `exit 0 on PASS, 2 on any HALT`\n     (`grep -n '2 on any HALT' h-mad/scripts/h_mad_assemble_tdd.py` at `d90decc6`), and its\n     existing HALTs keep exit 2 (spec v1.4 FR-5 residual). The FR-5 Task rewrites that docstring\n     in `h_mad_assemble_tdd.py`, in the same commit, to say which HALT exits which way. A\n     grep-able sentence pins the rewrite, so a stale \"2 on any HALT\" fails a test.\n","total_lines":4391}},"locations":[]}
